      "name": "performance_vs_claim",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.55,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A meaningful axis (product delivering on its promise) that helps explain complaints vs praise. But several rows have no explicit 'claim' yet are confidently coded: the doll review is availability-driven with no product claim, and the music review has no claim, both forced into 'below_claim'. These invent a claim frame the text does not support, weakening groundedness."
    },
    {
      "name": "content_quality_aspect",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.75,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Distinguishes which quality dimension drives sentiment (writing, plot, production, etc.), a strong grouping axis for this query. Mostly well grounded: writing_style for the Lamott book and 'not_present' for the skincare/doll rows are apt. The music review coded 'plot_character' is a stretch (it critiques emotional depth, not plot/character), but tolerable as loose categorisation."
    },
    {
      "name": "value_expectation_outcome",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
      "source_groundedness": 0.65,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Value/expectation framing is relevant to complaints about worth. Grounded where price is discussed (Bicycle Thieves = meets_expectation). But the doll review coded 'waste_of_money' asserts a purchase/money outcome the text never mentions (item was never bought), an unsupported specific. Partial overlap with performance_vs_claim but distinct enough."
    },
    {
      "name": "recommendation_stance",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Clear, high-leverage axis directly tied to praise/complaint outcomes. Values are well grounded: 'Skip it' and 'not enough to recommend it' = warns_against; explicit recommendations captured correctly. The doll review as 'explicitly_warns_against' is a mild overread but defensible given the negative framing."
    },
    {
      "name": "emotional_intensity",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.5,
      "source_groundedness": 0.7,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "An ordinal intensity scale can add a modelling axis beyond the binary label, but it is heavily concentrated at value 3 and reads as a somewhat generic affect proxy, limiting analytical leverage. Values are plausible readings of tone (strongly negative reviews scored higher), so groundedness is acceptable though inherently subjective."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.78,
  "source_groundedness": 0.72,
  "rationale": "The added columns give several genuine, distinct axes (recommendation stance, quality aspect, claim performance, value outcome) that directly serve the 'what drives praise/complaints' query; only emotional_intensity is comparatively generic. Groundedness is generally sound as categorisation, but a recurring failure mode is forcing money/claim frames onto rows that lack them (notably the doll availability review coded as below_claim and waste_of_money), producing unsupported specifics. Overall the set meets both necessary conditions at a good-but-imperfect level."
